DAY 16 - PART A: THEORY
Services: ClusterIP, Selectors, and Endpoints
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. WHAT IS A SERVICE?
---------------------
A Service gives a group of pods:
  - one stable virtual IP (ClusterIP) that never changes
  - one stable DNS name
  - load balancing across the healthy pods

It finds its pods with a LABEL SELECTOR (same idea as ReplicaSets).


2. A ClusterIP SERVICE
----------------------
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: Service
  metadata:
    name: web
  spec:
    type: ClusterIP          # default; reachable only inside cluster
    selector:
      app: web               # send traffic to pods with this label
    ports:
    - name: http
      port: 80               # port of the Service
      targetPort: 8080       # port the container listens on

port vs targetPort:
  clients connect to   web:80
  traffic goes to      <pod-ip>:8080
targetPort can also be a NAMED port from the pod spec (e.g. "http").


3. ENDPOINTSLICES
-----------------
The Service controller watches pods matching the selector and keeps a
list of their IP:port in EndpointSlice objects.
  - only READY pods are listed (readiness probes, Week 5)
  - pods come and go -> list updates automatically

  k get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web

(Older object "Endpoints" still exists but is deprecated.)


4. HOW TRAFFIC REALLY FLOWS
---------------------------
The ClusterIP is VIRTUAL: no interface has it, nothing listens on it.
kube-proxy on every node watches Services and EndpointSlices and writes
iptables (or IPVS/nftables) rules:

  "packets to 10.96.45.12:80  ->  DNAT to one of
                                  10.244.1.5:8080
                                  10.244.2.7:8080"

So the load balancing happens on the CLIENT's node, in the kernel.
Same DNAT mechanism as Docker's -p (Day 4). Day 18 shows the rules.


5. USEFUL OPTIONS
-----------------
sessionAffinity: ClientIP     same client -> same pod
Headless Service (clusterIP: None)
                              no virtual IP; DNS returns pod IPs
                              directly (used by StatefulSets, Week 4)
Service without selector      you manage endpoints yourself (e.g. to
                              point at an external database)
ExternalName                  DNS alias (CNAME) to an outside name


6. SERVICE TYPES OVERVIEW
-------------------------
  ClusterIP      inside the cluster only (default)
  NodePort       + a port on every node        (Day 18)
  LoadBalancer   + a cloud load balancer       (Day 18, Week 7)
  ExternalName   DNS alias only


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Service = stable IP + DNS + load balancing, via label selector.
- port = Service port; targetPort = container port.
- EndpointSlices list ready pod IPs; kube-proxy turns that into rules.
- ClusterIP is virtual; you can't ping it.
